deck "Small Systems, Clear Thinking" (
  author: "Ada Example"
  aspect: 16:9
  canvas: 1600x900
  theme: "paper"
  language: en
)

slide opening "Small systems, clear thinking" (
  background: "paper"
  transition: none
) """
\node[
  anchor=north west,
  text width=7cm,
  style=display
] (headline) at (1,8) {
Small systems,\\
clear thinking.
};

\node[
  anchor=north west,
  text width=6cm,
  style=lead
] (introduction) at (1,5) {
Good tools make the **important thing** obvious—and everything else quiet.

<div class="principle">
  Text, layout, and intent stay together.
</div>
};

\node[draw, rounded corners] (source) at (11,7) {Source};
\node[draw, rounded corners] (model) at (11,5) {Model};
\node[draw, rounded corners] (canvas) at (11,3) {Canvas};

\draw[->] (source) -- (model);
\draw[->] (model) -- (canvas);
\draw[->, dashed, bend left=35]
  (canvas.east) to node[right] {verified edit} (source.east);
"""

note """
Welcome everyone.

The diagram and prose belong to the same slide source. Each `\node` has a stable
identity and native TikZ properties. A node body may contain **Markdown**, HTML,
and LaTeX such as \(s \rightarrow s'\).
"""

slide questions "Start with better questions" (
  background: "paper"
  transition: fade
) """
\node[
  anchor=north west,
  text width=7cm,
  style=body
] (questions-list) at (1,8) {
Before adding a feature, ask:

1. **Who is it for?**
2. **What should become easier?**
3. **What must never be lost?**
};

\node[
  anchor=north west,
  text width=5cm,
  draw,
  rounded corners,
  rotate=-2
] (north-star) at (10,6) {
<blockquote>Preserve the author’s work first.</blockquote>
};

\node[anchor=north west, text width=6cm] (source-law) at (9,3) {
The source-preservation condition is
\[
  \operatorname{commit}(s,e)=s'
  \Longrightarrow
  \operatorname{parse}(s')=\operatorname{intent}(e).
\]
};

\draw[thick, ->] (questions-list.east) -- (north-star.west);
"""

note """
Pause on the third question.

For SlideBlaster, authored source is durable truth. The canvas is a view and an
editing surface, but a canvas operation commits only after reparsing proves the
intended result.

<span class="presenter-cue">Ask the audience for one failure example.</span>
"""

slide finish "Make the path complete" (
  background: "ink"
  foreground: "paper"
  transition: none
) """
\node[
  anchor=north west,
  text width=8cm,
  style=body-large
] (checklist) at (1,8) {
Build the smallest complete path:

- open the real document;
- make one meaningful change;
- verify the result;
- keep undo exact;
- let the user see what happened.
};

\node[
  anchor=north west,
  text width=4cm,
  draw,
  very thick,
  rounded corners,
  style=accent-card
] (takeaway) at (11,5) {
<strong>Make it real.</strong>

Then make it broader.
};

\draw[paper, very thick, ->] (checklist.east) -- (takeaway.west);
"""

note """
End on the distinction between foundations and vertical slices:

- a foundation proves that pieces exist;
- a vertical slice proves that a person can complete a real task.

The final arrow is TikZ; this list is Markdown; both remain authored source.
"""
